<p>Walking in to the room was an overwhelming experience. The triage nurses in the lobby of the Accident and Emergency unit took a few brief details then asked us to check in using the iPads on the wall just around the corner. As we turned the corner, it hit us that the waiting room was absolutely full. All the seats were taken, and people leaned against the walls and in the passageways, so that they had to shift every time someone went past. It was around 23:30 on a Tuesday. At this point, we had no idea that we would still be here nine hours later.</p><p>A few hours earlier, Mr. Bsag had been making dinner, when a pan full of boiling water fell, soaking his bare left food and splashing his right hand and wrist. He is still not quite clear how it happened, but the end result was a severely scalded foot and wrist. We did all the first aid things (running under cool water for much longer than you think is neccessary, then covering with a piece of clean cling film), then debated whether this was something we needed to go to A&amp;E for. I’m fairly confident about treating and dressing small wounds, but burns and scalds are something else, and this was a large area that was already starting to blister.</p><p>I want it to be clear that I’m an enormous supporter of the NHS. No part of our experience was the fault of the NHS staff, who are all over-worked and under-staffed. I also feel lucky that we could get treated without worrying that it would land us with a bill of thousands (or tens of thousands) of pounds. However, everyone knows that the NHS is under huge strain from years of under-funding, and as a result, no-one wants to have to go to A&amp;E. We had to go though, that was clear. As we sat in the taxi, we reassured each other that it was a Tuesday night, not a Friday or weekend night, so surely they wouldn’t be too busy. As you have read, we were very wrong about that.</p><p>After we had checked in, the sense of disorientation set in. Where should we sit? Was there somewhere that we could be out of the way? If the scald wasn’t enough, Mr. B’s AuDHD alarm bells were already clanging from overwhelm in this busy, noisy, unfamiliar room. We had to stand for a while, but then some seats were vacated as someone got called in for treatment, and we took them.</p><p>Waiting rooms are odd social spaces at the best of times, but A&amp;E waiting rooms are strange, almost otherworldly spaces. I’m reading ‘Orbital’ by Samantha Harvey at the moment, in which she describes the lives of six astronauts living in an orbital space station around Earth. She captures, in a way that feels very authentic (though I have no personal experience of living in an orbital space station), the simultaneous feelings of loneliness and claustrophobia that it generates. Sitting in the waiting room felt a bit like that, except without the zero gravity and stunning views of the Earth. Everyone was alone in their own little world of pain and fear, but had to negotiate their existence in the space with the others there. Everyone desperately wanted to be out of the room.</p><p>Time dragged on. There is no indication of where you are in the queue, or how long you might have to wait. No automated “Patient number 117, please” announcements. Everyone was alert to the doors opening at either end of the long room, through which medics appeared at intevals and called out a name, shouting to be heard over the hubub of misery. Heads went up like meerkats in the desert, then drooped again in disappointment as someone else’s name was called. What made it worse is that everybody had a couple of false alarms. Their name would be called, but they were just called to the lobby to get their vitals checked, or to another room for a secondary triage, each time, having to return to the same room to wait again, but now their seats were gone so they had to look for another.</p><p>As the night went on, I started to look around (as subtly as I could) at the others in the room. Some people were wired and loud. A young man (early 20’s?) had what looked like a teatowel pressed against a wound to his mouth, but carried on endless, ebullient conversations with friends on speakerphone about his predicament. Under the jokey bravado, I could hear the fear in the repeated playing down of what the eventual treatment would be like. At some point, he managed to eat a bag of salt and vinegar flavoured crisps from the vending machine though, so fair play to him. A woman who was brought in by ambulance sat in a hospital wheelchair, eyes pressed closed against the pain, her body drawing in on itself in an attempt to escape this room, this pain. Others shifted repeatedly with discomfort, boredom, anxiety, talking quietly with the person accompanying them, or trying to get comfortable enough to doze for a few minutes. An older man leant on his wooden cane in front of him, but each time he fell asleep, the cane clattered to the floor, startling him and the rest of the room. Throughout, everybody tried not to hear other people vomiting into the provided bags.</p><p>You go though phases. The period from about 3 to 4am was a bad one. We both felt trapped, irrationally feeling as if we were going to be stuck in this strip-lit purgatory for the rest of our lives. We really wanted to go, but this was the place to get treated, to start the healing process, to stave off infection. At around 05:20, the sun rose, and — inexplicably — it felt better. We were lightheaded with lack of sleep and hungry, but the sun coming in through the windows behind us felt hopeful. Surely Mr. B’s name would be called soon and we could go home?</p><p>07:00. 08:00. Hope faded again. We both started worrying about our 20 year old cat, Bianca. She had been on her own all night and would need feeding and her litter box cleaning out. We decided that I would pop back home and see to her, then if he hadn’t yet been seen, I would come back to the hospital and wait with him. Thankfully, by the time I had got home and sorted out Bianca, he texted me (along with a truly gruesome photo of his unwrapped foot) to say that he had been seen, his wounds dressed, my first aid efforts praised by the doctor, and he was heading back in a taxi. It was over.</p><p>We had more stress to come, because this was the day before his mother’s funeral, but now his foot is healing nicely and doesn’t hurt anymore. That night feels like some kind of out-of-body experience, like finding yourself orbiting the earth when you didn’t expect to be.</p>